  "slug": "bifurcation-topic-8fba",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply E/I circuit, mean-field, and surrogate-accelerated models to recovery dynamics and regime control in cortex.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is how circuit structure and plasticity rules set the dynamical regimes and recovery trajectories of cortical networks. A minimal E/I model with BCM metaplasticity and homeostasis explains differential amblyopia recovery under TTX versus reverse occlusion. Dynamical mean-field theory shows that cell-type-specific modularity in balanced E/I networks controls dynamical regimes and computational capabilities. A HyperNEAT-based generator produces age-specific connectomes whose recurrent controllers keep visuomotor behavior stable across development. In a multiscale macaque V1 model, deep network surrogates accelerate simulation roughly 10^5-fold, enabling stability analysis. What is new is the finding that similar visual tuning can conceal different recovery dynamics, shifting emphasis from matched outputs to the parameter-dependent trajectories beneath them.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
